1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,387
It's five-thirty on a weeknight in nineteen

2
00:00:02,387 --> 00:00:03,053
fifty-eight.

3
00:00:03,233 --> 00:00:05,517
In millions of American kitchens,

4
00:00:05,517 --> 00:00:08,147
something is bubbling on the stove and

5
00:00:08,147 --> 00:00:10,501
something is browning in the oven.

6
00:00:10,681 --> 00:00:12,725
The table is being set with the

7
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:13,781
everyday dishes.

8
00:00:13,961 --> 00:00:16,105
A child is sent to call the

9
00:00:16,105 --> 00:00:17,931
others in from outside.

10
00:00:18,111 --> 00:00:19,781
And at six o'clock sharp,

11
00:00:19,781 --> 00:00:22,186
the whole family sits down together.

12
00:00:22,366 --> 00:00:23,643
It's time for dinner.

13
00:00:23,994 --> 00:00:25,688
In nineteen fifty-eight,

14
00:00:25,688 --> 00:00:28,301
dinner was the center of family life.

15
00:00:28,481 --> 00:00:31,592
Most families ate together every single night.

16
00:00:31,772 --> 00:00:34,195
And what they ate was remarkably similar

17
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:35,406
from coast to coast.

18
00:00:35,586 --> 00:00:37,872
Meat, potatoes, a vegetable,

19
00:00:37,872 --> 00:00:39,259
bread and butter,

20
00:00:39,259 --> 00:00:40,892
and often a dessert.

21
00:00:41,072 --> 00:00:43,359
Mothers planned the menus a week at

22
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:45,515
a time, and many families ate the

23
00:00:45,515 --> 00:00:47,410
same thing on the same night,

24
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:48,456
week after week.

25
00:00:48,806 --> 00:00:50,744
So let's sit down at the table.

26
00:00:50,924 --> 00:00:53,090
Let's go through a typical week of

27
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,129
dinners in nineteen fifty-eight,

28
00:00:55,129 --> 00:00:56,085
night by night,

29
00:00:56,085 --> 00:00:57,996
and see what was on the plate,

30
00:00:57,996 --> 00:00:58,824
what it cost,

31
00:00:58,824 --> 00:01:00,608
and why it tasted the way it

32
00:01:00,608 --> 00:01:00,863
did.

33
00:01:01,043 --> 00:01:02,844
If you grew up in the fifties,

34
00:01:02,844 --> 00:01:04,645
I'll bet you can smell some of

35
00:01:04,645 --> 00:01:05,605
these right now.

36
00:01:05,956 --> 00:01:07,442
First, the shopping.

37
00:01:07,622 --> 00:01:10,047
Most mothers did one big trip to

38
00:01:10,047 --> 00:01:12,017
the supermarket each week,

39
00:01:12,017 --> 00:01:14,291
usually on Thursday or Friday,

40
00:01:14,291 --> 00:01:17,246
when the newspaper ran the grocery ads.

41
00:01:17,426 --> 00:01:19,398
They brought a list and a little

42
00:01:19,398 --> 00:01:20,445
stack of coupons.

43
00:01:20,625 --> 00:01:24,136
Many supermarkets gave out trading stamps,

44
00:01:24,136 --> 00:01:26,310
like S and H Green Stamps,

45
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:29,906
which families pasted into books and traded

46
00:01:29,906 --> 00:01:31,829
for toasters and lamps.

47
00:01:32,179 --> 00:01:34,447
Let's start with the most important meal

48
00:01:34,447 --> 00:01:35,127
of the week.

49
00:01:35,307 --> 00:01:36,271
Sunday dinner.

50
00:01:36,451 --> 00:01:38,931
After church, the family came home to

51
00:01:38,931 --> 00:01:41,142
a pot roast that had been cooking

52
00:01:41,142 --> 00:01:43,220
slowly in the oven all morning.

53
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:44,430
A chuck roast,

54
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:46,197
browned in a Dutch oven,

55
00:01:46,197 --> 00:01:49,657
with onions, carrots and potatoes tucked around

56
00:01:49,657 --> 00:01:49,878
it.

57
00:01:50,058 --> 00:01:52,162
By the time the family walked in,

58
00:01:52,162 --> 00:01:54,331
the whole house smelled wonderful.

59
00:01:54,681 --> 00:01:57,348
Sunday dinner was often eaten in the

60
00:01:57,348 --> 00:02:00,534
early afternoon, around one or two o'clock,

61
00:02:00,534 --> 00:02:02,239
and it was a big event.

62
00:02:02,419 --> 00:02:04,381
The good tablecloth came out.

63
00:02:04,561 --> 00:02:07,812
Grandparents, aunts and uncles came over.

64
00:02:07,992 --> 00:02:10,735
There was gravy made from the drippings,

65
00:02:10,735 --> 00:02:12,793
homemade rolls, and a pie or a

66
00:02:12,793 --> 00:02:13,959
cake for dessert.

67
00:02:14,139 --> 00:02:16,338
Children wore their church clothes,

68
00:02:16,338 --> 00:02:18,098
and were expected to behave.

69
00:02:18,448 --> 00:02:21,269
Some families had roast chicken or fried

70
00:02:21,269 --> 00:02:23,103
chicken on Sunday instead.

71
00:02:23,283 --> 00:02:24,328
In the fifties,

72
00:02:24,328 --> 00:02:26,348
chicken was a bit of a treat.

73
00:02:26,528 --> 00:02:28,949
Families bought a whole bird and cut

74
00:02:28,949 --> 00:02:30,092
it up themselves.

75
00:02:30,272 --> 00:02:32,468
Mothers fried it in a cast iron

76
00:02:32,468 --> 00:02:34,450
skillet with lard or Crisco,

77
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:36,929
and every family had its own recipe

78
00:02:36,929 --> 00:02:39,549
that nobody else was allowed to know.

79
00:02:39,899 --> 00:02:41,315
Monday was washday,

80
00:02:41,315 --> 00:02:43,625
the hardest day of the week for

81
00:02:43,625 --> 00:02:44,519
a housewife.

82
00:02:44,699 --> 00:02:47,555
So Monday dinner was usually something easy.

83
00:02:47,735 --> 00:02:49,407
Leftovers from Sunday.

84
00:02:49,587 --> 00:02:52,324
The pot roast became hot roast beef

85
00:02:52,324 --> 00:02:54,045
sandwiches with gravy,

86
00:02:54,045 --> 00:02:55,531
or roast beef hash,

87
00:02:55,531 --> 00:02:58,580
chopped up with potatoes and onions and

88
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:00,535
fried crisp in a skillet.

89
00:03:00,886 --> 00:03:04,157
Nothing was wasted in a nineteen fifty-eight

90
00:03:04,157 --> 00:03:04,752
kitchen.

91
00:03:04,932 --> 00:03:08,436
Leftover chicken became chicken pot pie or

92
00:03:08,436 --> 00:03:10,272
chicken and dumplings.

93
00:03:10,452 --> 00:03:12,031
The bones became soup.

94
00:03:12,211 --> 00:03:15,450
Stale bread became bread pudding or stuffing.

95
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:17,836
Bacon grease was saved in a coffee

96
00:03:17,836 --> 00:03:18,939
can by the stove.

97
00:03:19,119 --> 00:03:21,871
Mothers who had lived through the Depression

98
00:03:21,871 --> 00:03:24,622
and wartime rationing never threw food away.

99
00:03:24,973 --> 00:03:27,844
Monday was also soup night in many

100
00:03:27,844 --> 00:03:28,351
homes.

101
00:03:28,531 --> 00:03:30,426
A big pot simmered on the back

102
00:03:30,426 --> 00:03:31,752
of the stove all day,

103
00:03:31,752 --> 00:03:34,278
while the wash went through the wringer.

104
00:03:34,458 --> 00:03:36,149
Vegetable beef soup,

105
00:03:36,149 --> 00:03:38,347
split pea with a ham bone,

106
00:03:38,347 --> 00:03:39,868
or chicken noodle.

107
00:03:40,048 --> 00:03:43,108
Served with crackers or a grilled cheese

108
00:03:43,108 --> 00:03:44,791
sandwich, it was warm,

109
00:03:44,791 --> 00:03:45,939
cheap and easy,

110
00:03:45,939 --> 00:03:48,616
exactly what a tired mother needed.

111
00:03:48,967 --> 00:03:50,917
Tuesday might be casserole night.

112
00:03:51,097 --> 00:03:53,128
The fifties were the golden age of

113
00:03:53,128 --> 00:03:53,965
the casserole.

114
00:03:54,145 --> 00:03:56,585
And the secret ingredient in many of

115
00:03:56,585 --> 00:03:58,890
them was a can of Campbell's cream

116
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:00,043
of mushroom soup.

117
00:04:00,223 --> 00:04:01,391
Mixed with tuna,

118
00:04:01,391 --> 00:04:02,924
egg noodles and peas,

119
00:04:02,924 --> 00:04:05,625
and topped with crushed potato chips,

120
00:04:05,625 --> 00:04:07,962
it became tuna noodle casserole,

121
00:04:07,962 --> 00:04:10,298
one of the most famous dishes of

122
00:04:10,298 --> 00:04:11,101
the decade.

123
00:04:11,451 --> 00:04:14,425
There was also the green bean casserole,

124
00:04:14,425 --> 00:04:17,473
invented in nineteen fifty-five by a home

125
00:04:17,473 --> 00:04:20,968
economist named Dorcas Reilly in the Campbell's

126
00:04:20,968 --> 00:04:21,934
test kitchen.

127
00:04:22,114 --> 00:04:25,462
Green beans, cream of mushroom soup and

128
00:04:25,462 --> 00:04:27,780
crispy fried onions on top.

129
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:29,856
It was meant to be an easy

130
00:04:29,856 --> 00:04:31,315
weeknight side dish.

131
00:04:31,495 --> 00:04:34,470
Today, millions of families still make it

132
00:04:34,470 --> 00:04:35,849
every Thanksgiving.

133
00:04:36,199 --> 00:04:37,660
Casseroles were cheap,

134
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:39,986
filling and could feed a big family

135
00:04:39,986 --> 00:04:41,647
with a pound of meat or a

136
00:04:41,647 --> 00:04:42,445
can of tuna.

137
00:04:42,625 --> 00:04:44,401
They were also easy to carry.

138
00:04:44,581 --> 00:04:46,368
When a neighbor was sick,

139
00:04:46,368 --> 00:04:48,084
had a new baby or lost a

140
00:04:48,084 --> 00:04:50,801
loved one, a casserole appeared on the

141
00:04:50,801 --> 00:04:53,375
doorstep, in a dish with the owner's

142
00:04:53,375 --> 00:04:55,519
name taped to the bottom so it

143
00:04:55,519 --> 00:04:56,806
could be returned.

144
00:04:57,157 --> 00:04:58,898
Wednesday might be meatloaf.

145
00:04:59,078 --> 00:05:02,259
Ground beef, bread crumbs or oatmeal,

146
00:05:02,259 --> 00:05:04,150
an egg, chopped onion,

147
00:05:04,150 --> 00:05:06,729
and ketchup or a can of tomato

148
00:05:06,729 --> 00:05:09,566
soup, all mixed by hand and baked

149
00:05:09,566 --> 00:05:10,769
in a loaf pan.

150
00:05:10,949 --> 00:05:13,651
Some mothers put strips of bacon on

151
00:05:13,651 --> 00:05:15,967
top, or a glaze of ketchup and

152
00:05:15,967 --> 00:05:16,894
brown sugar.

153
00:05:17,074 --> 00:05:20,609
Served with mashed potatoes and canned green

154
00:05:20,609 --> 00:05:21,091
beans.

155
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:23,547
Ground beef was one of the cheapest

156
00:05:23,547 --> 00:05:24,691
meats in the store.

157
00:05:24,871 --> 00:05:26,742
In nineteen fifty-eight,

158
00:05:26,742 --> 00:05:29,237
a pound cost around fifty cents.

159
00:05:29,417 --> 00:05:32,143
Mothers stretched it as far as it

160
00:05:32,143 --> 00:05:33,960
could go, in meatloaf,

161
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:36,355
Salisbury steak, sloppy joes,

162
00:05:36,355 --> 00:05:40,733
stuffed peppers, Swedish meatballs and spaghetti with

163
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:41,642
meat sauce.

164
00:05:41,822 --> 00:05:44,351
And the leftovers made a great meatloaf

165
00:05:44,351 --> 00:05:46,814
sandwich for Dad's lunch the next day.

166
00:05:47,164 --> 00:05:49,208
And there was liver and onions.

167
00:05:49,388 --> 00:05:51,817
Many children of the fifties remember it

168
00:05:51,817 --> 00:05:52,546
with horror.

169
00:05:52,726 --> 00:05:55,068
Liver was cheap and full of iron,

170
00:05:55,068 --> 00:05:58,403
and doctors and home economists recommended it.

171
00:05:58,583 --> 00:06:01,267
Mothers fried it with onions and bacon,

172
00:06:01,267 --> 00:06:03,195
and served it once a week or

173
00:06:03,195 --> 00:06:03,401
so.

174
00:06:03,581 --> 00:06:05,892
Kids pushed it around their plates,

175
00:06:05,892 --> 00:06:07,477
cut it into tiny pieces,

176
00:06:07,477 --> 00:06:09,061
and drank a lot of milk.

177
00:06:09,411 --> 00:06:11,402
Thursday might be the night to try

178
00:06:11,402 --> 00:06:12,221
something new.

179
00:06:12,401 --> 00:06:13,502
In the fifties,

180
00:06:13,502 --> 00:06:17,318
women's magazines like Good Housekeeping and Ladies'

181
00:06:17,318 --> 00:06:20,621
Home Journal printed new recipes every month.

182
00:06:20,801 --> 00:06:23,231
Betty Crocker's Picture Cook Book,

183
00:06:23,231 --> 00:06:25,661
first published in nineteen fifty,

184
00:06:25,661 --> 00:06:27,662
was in millions of kitchens.

185
00:06:27,842 --> 00:06:30,984
Mothers clipped recipes and filed them in

186
00:06:30,984 --> 00:06:32,823
a little tin recipe box.

187
00:06:33,173 --> 00:06:36,259
The new recipes often featured canned and

188
00:06:36,259 --> 00:06:37,388
packaged foods.

189
00:06:37,568 --> 00:06:40,023
Chicken a la king served in pastry

190
00:06:40,023 --> 00:06:40,528
shells.

191
00:06:40,708 --> 00:06:43,949
Ham slices with canned pineapple rings and

192
00:06:43,949 --> 00:06:45,492
maraschino cherries.

193
00:06:45,672 --> 00:06:48,354
Spaghetti from a can of Chef Boyardee.

194
00:06:48,534 --> 00:06:50,805
And for a hint of the exotic,

195
00:06:50,805 --> 00:06:52,607
chop suey or chow mein,

196
00:06:52,607 --> 00:06:54,801
made from a La Choy kit with

197
00:06:54,801 --> 00:06:56,603
crunchy canned noodles.

198
00:06:56,952 --> 00:06:58,589
And there was the TV dinner.

199
00:06:58,769 --> 00:07:02,124
Swanson had introduced its frozen turkey TV

200
00:07:02,124 --> 00:07:04,543
dinner in nineteen fifty-three,

201
00:07:04,543 --> 00:07:07,976
in an aluminum tray with three compartments.

202
00:07:08,156 --> 00:07:11,297
Turkey, cornbread dressing and gravy,

203
00:07:11,297 --> 00:07:13,334
peas and sweet potatoes.

204
00:07:13,514 --> 00:07:15,952
It cost about ninety-eight cents.

205
00:07:16,132 --> 00:07:18,137
On nights when Mom was busy,

206
00:07:18,137 --> 00:07:19,568
or out at a meeting,

207
00:07:19,568 --> 00:07:22,002
the kids got TV dinners on folding

208
00:07:22,002 --> 00:07:24,364
trays in front of the television.

209
00:07:24,713 --> 00:07:26,943
And the kitchen itself was a busy

210
00:07:26,943 --> 00:07:27,349
place.

211
00:07:27,529 --> 00:07:29,143
The electric mixer whirred.

212
00:07:29,323 --> 00:07:31,847
The pressure cooker hissed and rattled on

213
00:07:31,847 --> 00:07:33,939
the stove, cooking tough meat in a

214
00:07:33,939 --> 00:07:35,232
fraction of the time.

215
00:07:35,412 --> 00:07:37,421
A radio played on the counter.

216
00:07:37,601 --> 00:07:40,070
And children were put to work shelling

217
00:07:40,070 --> 00:07:41,564
peas, peeling potatoes,

218
00:07:41,564 --> 00:07:44,358
setting the table and stirring the pudding.

219
00:07:44,707 --> 00:07:47,544
Friday was fish night in millions of

220
00:07:47,544 --> 00:07:48,016
homes.

221
00:07:48,196 --> 00:07:48,955
At the time,

222
00:07:48,955 --> 00:07:51,356
Catholics were not allowed to eat meat

223
00:07:51,356 --> 00:07:52,051
on Fridays.

224
00:07:52,231 --> 00:07:55,484
But even many non-Catholic families had fish

225
00:07:55,484 --> 00:07:57,258
on Friday, out of habit.

226
00:07:57,438 --> 00:08:00,381
And in schools and cafeterias across the

227
00:08:00,381 --> 00:08:02,883
country, Friday meant fish sticks.

228
00:08:03,232 --> 00:08:05,520
Fish sticks were a brand new invention.

229
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:08,141
They were introduced by Birds Eye in

230
00:08:08,141 --> 00:08:11,396
nineteen fifty-three, and they became an instant

231
00:08:11,396 --> 00:08:11,667
hit.

232
00:08:11,847 --> 00:08:14,784
Breaded, frozen and ready in minutes,

233
00:08:14,784 --> 00:08:17,641
served with tartar sauce or ketchup.

234
00:08:17,821 --> 00:08:20,143
Kids who didn't like fish loved them.

235
00:08:20,323 --> 00:08:23,585
Other Friday favorites were salmon patties made

236
00:08:23,585 --> 00:08:24,903
from canned salmon,

237
00:08:24,903 --> 00:08:26,708
and tuna salad sandwiches.

238
00:08:27,057 --> 00:08:29,459
Friday might also be the night for

239
00:08:29,459 --> 00:08:30,872
macaroni and cheese,

240
00:08:30,872 --> 00:08:33,980
either baked from scratch with real cheddar,

241
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:35,463
or from the blue box.

242
00:08:35,643 --> 00:08:38,576
Kraft had introduced its boxed macaroni and

243
00:08:38,576 --> 00:08:41,236
cheese dinner in nineteen thirty-seven,

244
00:08:41,236 --> 00:08:42,737
during the Depression.

245
00:08:42,917 --> 00:08:44,042
By the fifties,

246
00:08:44,042 --> 00:08:45,241
it was a staple,

247
00:08:45,241 --> 00:08:46,441
cheap and quick,

248
00:08:46,441 --> 00:08:49,291
and children have loved it ever since.

249
00:08:49,640 --> 00:08:51,741
In the Midwest and the South,

250
00:08:51,741 --> 00:08:54,203
Friday might also mean a fish fry.

251
00:08:54,383 --> 00:08:58,360
Churches, taverns, fire halls and lodges served

252
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:00,645
all-you-can-eat fried fish,

253
00:09:00,645 --> 00:09:04,200
with coleslaw, rye bread and potato salad,

254
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:05,807
for a dollar or so.

255
00:09:05,987 --> 00:09:07,555
The whole family went,

256
00:09:07,555 --> 00:09:09,479
and it was as much a social

257
00:09:09,479 --> 00:09:10,620
event as a meal.

258
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,553
In Wisconsin, the Friday fish fry is

259
00:09:13,553 --> 00:09:16,001
still a beloved tradition today.

260
00:09:16,351 --> 00:09:18,720
Saturday night was often the easy night.

261
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:21,099
Hamburgers or hot dogs on the new

262
00:09:21,099 --> 00:09:23,764
backyard grill, if the weather was good.

263
00:09:23,944 --> 00:09:25,891
Baked beans from a can,

264
00:09:25,891 --> 00:09:28,683
potato chips, and corn on the cob

265
00:09:28,683 --> 00:09:29,529
in summer.

266
00:09:29,709 --> 00:09:31,954
Or Dad might bring home a pizza,

267
00:09:31,954 --> 00:09:34,691
which was just becoming popular in many

268
00:09:34,691 --> 00:09:37,217
parts of the country outside the big

269
00:09:37,217 --> 00:09:37,708
cities.

270
00:09:38,058 --> 00:09:40,643
Some families ate breakfast for dinner on

271
00:09:40,643 --> 00:09:41,588
Saturday night.

272
00:09:41,768 --> 00:09:44,078
Pancakes, eggs and bacon.

273
00:09:44,258 --> 00:09:45,547
Or they went out,

274
00:09:45,547 --> 00:09:46,381
to a diner,

275
00:09:46,381 --> 00:09:49,337
a drive-in, or a family restaurant with

276
00:09:49,337 --> 00:09:50,702
a children's menu.

277
00:09:50,882 --> 00:09:52,551
In nineteen fifty-eight,

278
00:09:52,551 --> 00:09:54,498
going out to eat was still a

279
00:09:54,498 --> 00:09:56,862
treat, maybe once a month for many

280
00:09:56,862 --> 00:09:57,488
families.

281
00:09:57,668 --> 00:10:00,629
A family of five might spend five

282
00:10:00,629 --> 00:10:01,975
or six dollars.

283
00:10:02,325 --> 00:10:04,369
Regional dinners were different,

284
00:10:04,369 --> 00:10:04,624
too.

285
00:10:04,804 --> 00:10:05,880
In New England,

286
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:08,890
Saturday night meant baked beans and brown

287
00:10:08,890 --> 00:10:09,320
bread.

288
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:10,448
In the South,

289
00:10:10,448 --> 00:10:12,344
it might be fried catfish,

290
00:10:12,344 --> 00:10:14,458
collard greens and cornbread.

291
00:10:14,638 --> 00:10:17,320
In Texas, chili or barbecue.

292
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:20,716
In Pennsylvania, pork and sauerkraut.

293
00:10:20,896 --> 00:10:24,164
Immigrant families kept their own recipes alive,

294
00:10:24,164 --> 00:10:26,547
from pierogi to lasagna to tamales,

295
00:10:26,547 --> 00:10:29,406
and slowly shared them with the neighbors.

296
00:10:29,755 --> 00:10:31,474
Every dinner had sides.

297
00:10:31,654 --> 00:10:33,740
And in nineteen fifty-eight,

298
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:36,124
many vegetables came from a can.

299
00:10:36,304 --> 00:10:38,199
Canned peas, canned corn,

300
00:10:38,199 --> 00:10:41,079
canned green beans and canned carrots,

301
00:10:41,079 --> 00:10:43,429
heated in a saucepan with a pat

302
00:10:43,429 --> 00:10:44,187
of butter.

303
00:10:44,367 --> 00:10:47,548
Frozen vegetables were growing more popular too.

304
00:10:47,728 --> 00:10:50,584
In summer, fresh vegetables came from the

305
00:10:50,584 --> 00:10:51,072
garden.

306
00:10:51,421 --> 00:10:52,838
And then there was Jell-O.

307
00:10:53,018 --> 00:10:55,017
The fifties were the height of the

308
00:10:55,017 --> 00:10:55,781
Jell-O salad.

309
00:10:55,961 --> 00:10:59,525
Lime Jell-O with shredded carrots and pineapple.

310
00:10:59,705 --> 00:11:03,746
Cherry Jell-O with fruit cocktail suspended inside.

311
00:11:03,926 --> 00:11:06,568
Some even had cottage cheese,

312
00:11:06,568 --> 00:11:08,117
celery or olives.

313
00:11:08,297 --> 00:11:10,506
They were molded in fancy shapes and

314
00:11:10,506 --> 00:11:12,163
served on a bed of lettuce.

315
00:11:12,343 --> 00:11:14,942
Children either loved them or hid them

316
00:11:14,942 --> 00:11:16,105
in their napkins.

317
00:11:16,454 --> 00:11:18,115
Bread was white bread,

318
00:11:18,115 --> 00:11:19,699
usually Wonder Bread,

319
00:11:19,699 --> 00:11:20,907
sliced and soft,

320
00:11:20,907 --> 00:11:22,794
with butter or margarine.

321
00:11:22,974 --> 00:11:25,440
Milk came in glass bottles from the

322
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:28,119
milkman, and every child drank a glass

323
00:11:28,119 --> 00:11:28,964
with dinner.

324
00:11:29,144 --> 00:11:31,871
And many families said grace before every

325
00:11:31,871 --> 00:11:34,531
meal, with heads bowed and hands folded.

326
00:11:34,881 --> 00:11:35,822
And for dessert?

327
00:11:36,002 --> 00:11:38,997
Many families had something sweet every night.

328
00:11:39,177 --> 00:11:41,209
A slice of homemade pie.

329
00:11:41,389 --> 00:11:43,781
A piece of cake from a Betty

330
00:11:43,781 --> 00:11:46,172
Crocker or Duncan Hines mix.

331
00:11:46,352 --> 00:11:47,386
Rice pudding.

332
00:11:47,566 --> 00:11:48,982
Canned peaches.

333
00:11:49,162 --> 00:11:51,279
Or a bowl of ice cream,

334
00:11:51,279 --> 00:11:53,765
Neapolitan, with chocolate,

335
00:11:53,765 --> 00:11:56,894
vanilla and strawberry in one box.

336
00:11:57,244 --> 00:11:59,904
Children who didn't clean their plates often

337
00:11:59,904 --> 00:12:01,053
didn't get dessert.

338
00:12:01,233 --> 00:12:04,201
Think of the starving children in Europe,

339
00:12:04,201 --> 00:12:06,228
or China, mothers would say,

340
00:12:06,228 --> 00:12:08,907
about the vegetables left on a plate.

341
00:12:09,087 --> 00:12:12,024
Kids argued, bargained and tried to feed

342
00:12:12,024 --> 00:12:14,300
their peas to the dog under the

343
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:14,741
table.

344
00:12:14,921 --> 00:12:16,465
It almost never worked.

345
00:12:16,814 --> 00:12:18,475
What did all this cost?

346
00:12:18,655 --> 00:12:20,306
In nineteen fifty-eight,

347
00:12:20,306 --> 00:12:23,126
the average American family spent about a

348
00:12:23,126 --> 00:12:25,052
fifth of its income on food.

349
00:12:25,232 --> 00:12:27,693
Today, it's around one tenth.

350
00:12:27,873 --> 00:12:30,104
Food was a bigger share of the

351
00:12:30,104 --> 00:12:33,079
budget then, which is one reason mothers

352
00:12:33,079 --> 00:12:34,269
were so careful,

353
00:12:34,269 --> 00:12:37,170
so creative, and so determined to waste

354
00:12:37,170 --> 00:12:37,765
nothing.

355
00:12:38,114 --> 00:12:39,882
But the real meaning of dinner in

356
00:12:39,882 --> 00:12:41,864
nineteen fifty-eight wasn't the food.

357
00:12:42,044 --> 00:12:43,043
It was the table.

358
00:12:43,223 --> 00:12:44,438
The whole family,

359
00:12:44,438 --> 00:12:46,938
every night, talking about the day.

360
00:12:47,118 --> 00:12:49,510
Dad telling stories from work.

361
00:12:49,690 --> 00:12:51,819
Kids arguing about whose turn it was

362
00:12:51,819 --> 00:12:52,824
to do the dishes.

363
00:12:53,004 --> 00:12:55,942
Mom asking if anyone wanted seconds.

364
00:12:56,122 --> 00:12:57,059
For many people,

365
00:12:57,059 --> 00:12:59,577
those dinners are their warmest memories of

366
00:12:59,577 --> 00:13:00,162
childhood.

367
00:13:00,512 --> 00:13:03,682
Today, many families eat at different times,

368
00:13:03,682 --> 00:13:05,050
in different rooms,

369
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:07,211
in front of different screens.

370
00:13:07,391 --> 00:13:10,057
Maybe that's why so many people remember

371
00:13:10,057 --> 00:13:12,523
the dinners of the fifties so fondly.

372
00:13:12,703 --> 00:13:14,688
It wasn't always fancy food.

373
00:13:14,868 --> 00:13:16,320
But everybody was there.

374
00:13:16,669 --> 00:13:18,469
So tell us in the comments.

375
00:13:18,649 --> 00:13:20,507
What was your favorite dinner growing up?

376
00:13:20,687 --> 00:13:22,794
What did your mother make on Sunday,

377
00:13:22,794 --> 00:13:24,492
and what dish did you hate as

378
00:13:24,492 --> 00:13:24,843
a kid?

379
00:13:25,023 --> 00:13:26,506
We read every comment,

380
00:13:26,506 --> 00:13:28,123
and we love the recipes.

381
00:13:28,303 --> 00:13:30,324
Next time, we're going to the mall

382
00:13:30,324 --> 00:13:31,751
in nineteen eighty-four.

383
00:13:31,931 --> 00:13:33,671
Thank you for spending this time with

384
00:13:33,671 --> 00:13:33,812
me.
